{{History of Balisca}}
The '''Battle of Navassa''' ({{lang-ca|Batalla de Navaza}}), also known as the '''Battle of Forts Michael and St. Philip''' ({{lang-ca|Batalla dels forts Miquel i Sant Felip}}), fought between 24-26 November 1816, was the decisive battle for possession of [[Navassa]], the largest city still in the hands of Royalists during the [[Continuation War]]. Two Royalist-held forts defending [[The Narrows (Navassa)|the Narrows]] (the entrance to the [[Navassan Lagoon]]) northwest of the city were attacked by an assembled [[Confederal Navy]] fleet. As long as the forts could keep the rebel forces from moving on the city, it was safe, but if they were negated, there were no fall-back positions to impede the rebel advance.

Earlier, Bayaran ships had driven off an earlier Confederalist blockade fleet in the [[Battle of Aitona]] the previous October. The ensuing battle can be divided into two parts: a mostly-ineffective {{W|bombardment}} of the Volisan-held forts by the assembled Confederalist fleet, and the successful passage of the forts by much of Farragut's fleet on the night of April 24. 

During the passage, one Confederalist warship was lost and three others turned back, while the Iberian ships were virtually obliterated. The subsequent capture of the city, achieved with no further significant opposition, was a serious, if not fatal, blow from which the Volisan and Loyalist forces never recovered. The forts remained after the fleet had passed, and we later restored for use by the Baliscan rebels. 

